  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM Thursday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A pair of cold fronts will bring gusty winds and \npotentially a few light showers today and tomorrow. Frost and \npotentially a freeze will be possible Friday night. \n\nThe first cold front appears to move through the region Thursday \nafternoon into the evening. The chance for pre-frontal showers is \nstill low, with most models suggesting that most of the rain will \ndry up as the front crosses the mountains. Some very light rain may \nstill be possible early this morning ahead of the front, mainly in \nthe Triad, and this afternoon in the northern Piedmont into the \nnorthern Coastal Plain. The REFS is suggesting that only up to trace \namounts of rain are likely, with a lesser chance of 0.01 inches in a \nfew locations in the north. Highs this afternoon will be dependent \non the timing of the frontal passage, but look to max out in the \nupper 60s in the NW to the low/mid 70s in the SE. Winds should be \npretty gusty with and behind the frontal passage today, with \nnorthwesterly gusts between 25-30 mph likely this afternoon and \nevening. Minimum temperatures tonight should drop into the low to \nmid 40s, with a few cool spots in the upper 30s. \n\nAn additional reinforcing cold front should move through the region \non Friday. Ahead of the fropa, a few waves may move through the \nregion. This could allow for a few additional light showers Friday \nevening/night, however the chance remains low with the amount of dry \nair over the region. Regardless, gusty winds will be possible again \nFriday afternoon ahead of the front, with gusts between 20-25 mph \npossible. Lows Friday night will also dip into the 30s. This will be \nthe start of the coldest air so far in the season and will likely \nresult in some sort of frost/freeze products. It appears that the \natmosphere will not decouple which should help keep temperatures \nsomewhat warmer than it looks in future nights. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... The chilliest air of the season will arrive and \nsupport the first widespread frost/freeze risk this weekend, \nfollowed by continued below average temperatures (including \nadditional frost/freezes) and dry conditions that will persist \nthrough most of next week.\n\nA mid/upr-level cyclone will progress slowly across and offshore the \nNortheast and Atlantic Canada through the weekend, followed by the \nprogression of a high amplitude ridge from the cntl CONUS to the \nMiddle Atlantic next week. The models indicate the basal portion of \na vigorous trough now progressing across the ern N. Pacific will \nprogress beneath the ridge and may briefly form a closed low over OK \nby Sun, then deamplify while progressing ewd and across and offshore \nthe Southeast Mon-Wed. Mostly high-level cloudiness will \nprecede/accompany the latter feature and may hinder otherwise \nexcellent radiational cooling potential Mon morning. \n\nAt the surface, cP pressure will build from central Canada to the \nMiddle Atlantic this weekend through early next week, where it will \nremain while gradually weakening/modifying through most of next \nweek.  \n\nThe coldest temperatures of the period will probably result Sun \nmorning, by which time prior episodes of CAA will have waned, and \nthe MSL pressure gradient relaxed. Sub-freezing temperatures appear \nlikely, except for perhaps around freezing in the urban Triangle; \nand a hard freeze will be possible especially in outlying/rural \nPiedmont locations. While some moderation will result as the high \ngradually weakens and airmass modifies through most of next week, \nits continued presence and influence will yield continued below \naverage temperatures and multiple, consecutive mornings of \nfrost/freeze conditions.",
